#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161793 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162001 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6553801-8b7e-5584-940d-a85600b44ec6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: One Mode"}] :question/id #uuid "21160b89-bb32-5dc5-9a4a-f7589905c913" :question/math-academy-id "e-8241" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown below. What is the mode of $X$?\n\n![](images/13/137e06758f63232479a573ffd7c7c68cffd832d8fac22036bce04e9968ad7c4a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x)$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=7$.\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $7$.\n\n![](images/d5/d500063d0885dc2437d83754d8e54d81740a8b6fd1855416e51dab2577cb27a6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162002 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "469819e4-0596-5aba-944a-52609afc8af7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: Multiple Modes"}] :question/id #uuid "32f1849f-60e5-58bd-823a-f6bbbdb51153" :question/math-academy-id "e-8242" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X$?\n\n![](images/d6/d63a97b931fedb6b001f590134b448dd2222f33606725343486064cec5e85036.png)" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x)$.\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=1,7,13$.\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $1,7$, and $13$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4efdc7b16747f33e48be7c73369e4f881177d6faba19d02bc832bfa0e916278.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"}] :question/id #uuid "e1167d53-7cfb-5ec2-96df-250e226d60b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8243" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X$?\n\n${\\frac{3}{2}(x - 1)^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x)$.\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x)$, we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}(x-1)^2$. Taking the derivative, we get\n$f^{′}(x)|= \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2(x - 1); = 3(x - 1)$,\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$, we get\n$3(x-1)= 0\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1$.\nThis critical point is within the interval $[0,2]$, so we keep it.\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0, 2$. So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1, 2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0) &= \\frac{3}{2}(0 - 1)^{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\nf(1) &= \\frac{3}{2}(1 - 1)^{2} = 0 \\\\\nf(2) &= \\frac{3}{2}(2 - 1)^{2} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(0) = f(2) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $0$ and $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e51181c-2074-5d7d-a9b8-d6f6128bc44c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: One Mode"}] :question/id #uuid "8be540db-a27c-537c-a804-35ed7cc45168" :question/math-academy-id "e-8244" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X$?\n\n${\\frac{\\sin x}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le π \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x)$.\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x)$, we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x\\leq \\pi$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{2}$. Taking the derivative, we get\n$f^{′}(x)|= \\frac{\\cos x}{2}$,\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\cos x}{2} = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi$,\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only $x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ lies within the interval $\\left[0, \\pi \\right]$.\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0, \\pi$. So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points\n\n$$\nx=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0) &= \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 0 = 0 \\\\\nf(\\frac{π}{2}) &= \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nf(π) &= \\frac{1}{2}\\sin π = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a2055ab-f621-57e8-8ee5-eac96b5db9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1, 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161199} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e9d273-85ed-5216-8df5-35f4402ea821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161200} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7aef3c-76a2-5354-9203-a2ed784a790e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}, 0, \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161201} {:answer/id #uuid "0a912f12-e0b0-598e-a72f-b0bcff1a8feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2, 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161202} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1fe261-c48b-5ed1-a962-4bfcb24415b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2, 0, 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161203}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d05b0e67-0633-544d-b4ab-dfd417417c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161198}] :question/id #uuid "df132e5e-2e4d-5e3b-bfb1-3123d68290f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94958" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X$?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{15}{128}(4x^{2} - x^{4}),\\begin{vmatrix}-2 \\le x \\le 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$"}]}
